    {
      "field_name": "established_mRS_inferred",
      "value": "1",
      "quote": "She was eupneic, and the respiratory clinical examination was unremarkable, revealing physiological lung sounds and normal diaphragm excursion.",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "clinical features such as stiffness/spasms/ hyperlordosis, positive antibodies such as anti-GAD, EMG showing continuous motor-unit activity, response to benzodiazepines",
      "quote": "SPS is commonly associated with high anti-glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD) antibodies which may lead to a functional synaptic blockade of brain and spinal cord GABAergic neurons, and, in turn to motor cortex hyperexcitability and muscle hyperactivity. Our monitoring suggests a complex participation of several muscle groups to the development of the respiratory crisis. Abnormal untimely activation of laryngeal constrictors, like the thyroarytenoid muscles, could be responsible for upper airway obstruction and stridor [6]. At the same time, EMGs showed simultaneous activation of antagonist muscles, consisting of an abnormal, continuous motor units discharge in the abdominal expiratory muscles and of the sternocleidomastoid muscle, an accessory inspiratory muscle. Their activity was not in keeping with a regular alternation of inspiration and expiration. However, a respiratory rhythm was not completely lost, as demonstrated by the thoracic and abdominal movements, although they were out-of-phase as a consequence of the upper airway spasm. Nevertheless, the movements suggest the persistence of a residual respiratory activity of the diaphragm and, possibly, of intercostal muscles, that were unable to generate respiratory flow. Paroxysmal autonomic sympathetic hyperactivity can intervene during respiratory crises in SPS, and worsen patients' symptoms. Our patient had some degree of autonomic dysregulation, as demonstrated by paroxysmal episodes of arterial hypertension in the intercritical periods, but did not show prominent signs of sympathetic hyperactivity during the respiratory crises.\n\nOur patient is representative of a severe case of SPS, whose manifestation could lead to acute respiratory failure and, in extreme cases, to death [7]. However, severity and time course of respiratory symptoms in SPS reported in the literature largely vary [8]. Further observations on patients with SPS and respiratory symptoms could enrich our knowledge on the heterogeneity of the syndrome and, possibly, lead to a phenotypic characterization based on its respiratory manifestations.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
